<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad IV of Granada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1315">1315</a>–<a href="/years/1333">1333</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasrid-dynasty">Nasrid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad IV was Nasrid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> from 1325 to 1333, coming to the throne as a boy of ten. With the help of Marinid forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> he besieged and recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1333, but he was murdered shortly afterwards by discontented commanders. He was succeeded by his brother Yusuf I.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-iv-of-granada.jpg" alt="Muhammad IV of Granada" width="320" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:15th_century_depiction_of_Battle_of_Teba_1330.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad was born in the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> on 14 April 1315, the first son of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alodia">Alwa</a>, a Christian. On 8 July 1325 his father was assassinated by a relative, and the ten-year-old Muhammad was proclaimed sultan the same day. The wounded vizier Abu al-Hasan ibn Mas'ud rallied the court to swear allegiance, and his grandmother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a> bint al-Ahmar, through whom he descended from earlier sultans, lent his accession crucial support. The Castilian Chronicle of Alfonso XI claims the murder was secretly planned by Uthman ibn Abi al-Ula, commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volunteers-of-the-faith">Volunteers of the Faith</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In the first years ministers vied for control. Uthman and the vizier Muhammad ibn al-Mahruq fell out, and in January 1327 Uthman proclaimed Muhammad's uncle, Muhammad ibn Faraj, a rival sultan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> and won the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xi-of-castile">Alfonso XI of Castile</a>, who seized Olvera and other frontier fortresses. To obtain Marinid troops the court gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronda">Ronda</a>, Marbella and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a> to the Marinid Sultan Abu Said Uthman II. In 1328 Muhammad, now taking charge himself, reconciled with Uthman and had Ibn al-Mahruq killed, ending the civil war. In 1329 he made his former tutor Abu Nuaym Ridwan hajib, the first holder of that office among the Nasrids.</p><p>In 1330 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Aragon invaded together: Alfonso XI took Teba after defeating a Granadan army, and crusading forces ravaged the countryside. Muhammad made peace with Castile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> on 19 February 1331, agreeing to pay tribute, but Alfonso soon stopped the promised food exports, and war with Aragon went on. In September 1332 Muhammad sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, and the Marinid Sultan Abu al-Hasan Ali sent 5,000 troops under his son Abu Malik Abd al-Wahid. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> surrendered on 17 June 1333 after about five months of siege, and a truce of 24 August lifted Alfonso's siege of the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The next day, 25 August 1333, Muhammad was killed near the mouth of the Guadiaro on the orders of Uthman's sons Abu Thabit and Ibrahim; a slave named Zayyan carried out the killing. Muslim sources such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> blame the brothers' hostility to his Marinid alliance, while Castilian sources say he was killed for his friendliness with Alfonso XI. He was buried in Málaga, later under a domed mausoleum. Aged about 18, he left no descendants. His biographers describe him as a keen hunter and rider, interested in literature and poetry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad IV of Granada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Málaga, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.18,-3.6&amp;year=1326" aria-label="Granada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Muhammad IV of Granada"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4755 4384h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4755 4384h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.18,-3.6&amp;year=1326">What was here in 1326? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
